{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778907459968,"sessionID":"ses_1d0dbbbe0ffe42a1PocQYG02zH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_pNfQBRPn0tahzPFPzdRgekzH","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r4s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260516-001-r4s0\n+title: \"Fast-path load extract\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  Load retirement currently pays for a generic variable right-shift and\n+  sign-extension cone in the MEM stage, even though CoreMark's hot path is\n+  dominated by simple word and byte accesses. Tightening that alignment logic\n+  should shorten the dmem->wb->forward path and improve Fmax without changing\n+  architectural behavior.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Replace the generic load-data shifter in `mem_stage.sv` with a small\n+  byte/halfword select tree that extracts the requested lane directly from the\n+  memory word and only applies sign/zero extension for actual load widths.\n+  Keep store masking and RVFI reporting unchanged. The goal is to trim the\n+  combinational depth on load-use cycles, which should raise median Fmax on\n+  Tang Nano 20K with no ISA-visible tradeoff.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Replace the variable-shift load extraction path with direct byte/halfword selection and width-specific extension logic.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r4s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r4s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r4s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bo