{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778914337194,"sessionID":"ses_1d0725296ffe04hte1OIgK20rq","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_MWSwiagjw1f6qVnV6qruMWGY","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r6s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260516-001-r6s0\n+title: \"Opcode-aware load-use gating\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The load-use interlock still compares raw IF/ID register fields even when the\n+  younger instruction does not actually read rs1 or rs2. That creates avoidable\n+  one-cycle bubbles on immediates, jumps, fences, and SYSTEM encodings, and it\n+  keeps avoidable compare fanout on the PC-hold path.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a tiny operand-usage classifier inside the hazard unit so load-use stalls\n+  only fire when the younger instruction really reads the compared source. Keep\n+  forwarding, RVFI reporting, and the rest of the pipeline unchanged; only the\n+  false dependency check should disappear. This should recover wasted CoreMark\n+  cycles on payload-only register encodings with very low RTL risk.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Pass the full IF/ID instruction bundle into the hazard path so the interlock can classify live operands locally.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Classify rs1/rs2 usage from opcode/funct fields and gate load-use compares so payload-only fields do not stall the pipe.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r6s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r6s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-m