{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778922942823,"sessionID":"ses_1cfeed0e7ffe5UhfgzkZkwcr20","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_cHTyrvoplM7bkU1Es8iGvhqK","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260516-001-r7s0\n+title: \"Opcode-aware interlock trim\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The current load-use interlock still treats raw IF/ID register fields as live\n+  operands, even when the younger instruction does not actually read them. That\n+  creates avoidable bubbles on immediates, jumps, fences, and SYSTEM encodings\n+  while keeping a hot compare cone on the PC-hold path.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Teach the hazard unit to classify whether the younger instruction truly reads\n+  rs1 or rs2 before it checks the load-use dependency. By gating the compare on\n+  real operand usage, the core should stop stalling on payload-only register\n+  fields and slightly reduce stall-path fanout, improving CoreMark iter/cycle\n+  without changing architectural behavior.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Pass the full IF/ID instruction into the hazard path so the interlock can classify operand usage locally.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Decode rs1/rs2 usage from opcode and funct fields, then gate load-use stalls so only true operand readers can trigger them.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiment