{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778929148777,"sessionID":"ses_1cf90375cffejJzZkn94JtCiPA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_B3Z5fOzpUXrdZ9pYZnn5oGg9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r8s2.yaml","offset":1,"limit":240},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r8s2.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: id: hyp-20260516-003-r8s2\n2: title: \"Single-entry loop predictor\"\n3: category: predictor\n4: motivation: |\n5:   CoreMark keeps reusing a small number of hot backward branches, but the\n6:   broader predictor experiments already showed that extra BTB/RAS state can\n7:   erase the benefit through aliasing and fetch-side overhead. A very small\n8:   predictor that only remembers one hot loop backedge should be cheaper to\n9:   update and easier to time.\n10: hypothesis: |\n11:   Add a single-entry learned predictor in `rtl/if_stage.sv` that caches the\n12:   target and taken direction for the hottest backward conditional branch, then\n13:   steers fetch early when the same PC reappears. Do not add a general BTB, RAS,\n14:   or JALR speculation; keep EX as the recovery path for everything else. This\n15:   is narrower than the prior table-based attempts and should capture the\n16:   repeat-backedge behavior CoreMark actually exercises without the bookkeeping\n17:   that hurt Fmax.\n18: expected_impact:\n19:   fitness_delta_pct: 3\n20:   confidence: medium\n21: changes:\n22:   - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n23:     description: \"Add a one-entry learned predictor for the hottest backward conditional branch and use it to redirect fetch before EX resolves the loop backedge.\"\n\n(End of file - total 23 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260516-003-r8s2\ntitle: \"Single-entry loop predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  CoreMark keeps reusing a small number of hot backward branches, but the\n  broader predictor experiments already showed that extra BTB/RAS state can\n  erase the benefit through aliasing and fetch-side overhead. A very small\n  predictor that only remembers one hot loop backedge should be cheaper to\n  update and easier to time.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a single-entry learned predictor in `rtl/if_stage.sv` that caches the\n  target and taken direction for the hottest backward conditional branch, then\n  steers fetch early when the same PC reappears. Do not add a general BTB, RAS,\n  or JALR speculation; keep EX as the recovery path for everything else. This\n  is narrower than the prior table-based attempts and should capture the\n  repeat-backedge behavior CoreMark actually exercises without the bookkeeping\n  that hurt Fmax.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r8s2.yaml","time":{"start":1778929148752,"end":1778929148775}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_027600cac1150520016a084dfc6e2481989dd87f8f04dbb322"}},"id":"prt_e3070a2a1001JUGLXbfNA2x8T1","sessionID":"ses_1cf90375cffejJzZkn94JtCiPA","messageID":"msg_e307093a2001UXLsJb1nKKr1jJ"}}
