{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778929168368,"sessionID":"ses_1cf90375cffejJzZkn94JtCiPA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_SI0ZEwtT1sEBP4O84u5lQIwt","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r8s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260516-001-r8s0\n+title: \"Decoder-gated load-use trim\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The load-use interlock still compares raw register fields even when the\n+  younger instruction cannot actually read one of them. That creates avoidable\n+  one-cycle bubbles on immediates, jumps, fences, and SYSTEM ops, and it keeps\n+  a small but hot compare cone on the PC-hold path. Moving the operand-use\n+  decision into decode should recover those stalls without changing ISA\n+  behavior.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add decoder-derived rs1/rs2 usage bits and thread them only into the\n+  load-use hazard check. Keep forwarding, RVFI, and the rest of the pipeline\n+  unchanged; the only behavior change should be that payload-only register\n+  fields no longer trigger stalls. This should improve CoreMark iter/cycle a\n+  bit and slightly reduce the hazard fanout seen by Fmax.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add source-usage sideband bits to the pipeline bundle so decode can carry whether rs1 and rs2 are real operands.\"\n+  - file: rtl/decoder.sv\n+    description: \"Classify which decoded instruction forms actually read rs1 and rs2, alongside the existing control outputs.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the operand-usage bits with the decoded instruction so the hazard path can consume them without re-decoding.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the decoded source-usage sideband into the hazard unit while leaving the forwarding and RVFI plumbing unchanged.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Gate the load-use compare on real operand reads so immediates, jumps, fences, and SYSTEM ops do not stall on payload-only register fields.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r8s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r8s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r8s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r8s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@\n+id: hyp-20260516-001-r8s0\n+title: \"Decoder-gated load-use trim\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The load-use interlock still compares raw register fields even when the\n+  younger instruction cannot actually read one of them. That creates avoidable\n+  one-cycle bubbles on immediates, jumps, fences, and SYSTEM ops, and it keeps\n+  a small but hot compare cone on the PC-hold path. Moving the operand-use\n+  decision into decode should recover those stalls without changing ISA\n+  behavior.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add decoder-derived rs1/rs2 usage bits and thread them only into the\n+  load-use hazard check. Keep forwarding, RVFI, and the rest of the pipeline\n+  unchanged; the only behavior change should be that payload-only register\n+  fields no longer trigger stalls. This should improve CoreMark iter/cycle a\n+  bit and slightly reduce the hazard fanout seen by Fmax.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add source-usage sideband bits to the pipeline bundle so decode can carry whe