{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778907432467,"sessionID":"ses_1d0dbbacaffe2J4VVJ2VDqzxR3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_WvEOxIarIzLvmkgkrM6zdcCP","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-002-r4s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260516-002-r4s1\n+title: \"Split decode from read\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current ID stage still mixes decode, immediate generation, and regfile\n+  access in one cycle. After the registered IF/ID cutpoint, that combined cone\n+  is the next obvious timing hotspot, so splitting it should help Fmax without\n+  changing the architectural contract.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Move instruction decode and immediate/control extraction into the fetch-side\n+  registered bundle, then let ID focus on operand reads and latching only the\n+  already-predecoded metadata. This should shorten the ID-stage combinational\n+  path and reduce fanout from the instruction bits while keeping stall, flush,\n+  redirect, and RVFI behavior unchanged.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Expand the IF/ID bundle to carry predecoded control and immediate metadata alongside the fetched instruction.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Predecode the registered instruction and latch the derived control bundle with the fetch output.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Consume the predecoded bundle directly so ID only performs regfile reads and state capture.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-002-r4s1.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-002-r4s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bon