ers/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Registered Fetch/Decode Cutpoint\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current IF stage hands live instruction data straight into decode, so\\\\nthe fetch, decode, immediate generation, and regfile-read path stays on the\\\\nFPGA timing critical path every cycle. A real registered cutpoint should\\\\nshorten that combinational chain and raise Fmax without changing the in-order\\\\nretirement model.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current IF stage hands live instruction data straight into decode, so\\\\nthe fetch, decode, immediate generation, and regfile-read path stays on the\\\\nFPGA timing critical path every cycle. A real registered cutpoint should\\\\nshorten that combinational chain and raise Fmax without changing the in-order\\\\nretirement model.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Convert IF into a true registered stage that latches {pc, instr, valid} on\\\\nthe clock edge and feeds ID from that register instead of directly from\\\\nio_imemData. Keep the existing stall, flush, and redirect behavior intact so\\\\nthe architectural interface stays the same, but remove the live fetch-to-\\\\ndecode combinational dependency that likely limits median clock on Tang Nano\\\\n20K. The expected win is higher Fmax with no change to RVFI ordering or ISA\\\\nbehavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\"... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n4: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778914281289,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d07251c3ffeYWujJXZwFLWCpv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_6eZBOSpoNnmKWgtiUeo0gdx1\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":400},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Load-use interlocks should gate on decoder-derived rs1/rs2 usage bits, because raw register-field compares can falsely stall immediates, jumps, fences, and system ops on payload-only operand bits.\\n2: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +9.94%): Turning IF into a real registered IF/ID cutpoint raised Fmax by removing the live io_imemData-to-decode combinational chain, but the register has to own flush/redirect/bubble behavior so control-flow changes still land as NOPs.\\n3: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.29%): Static backward-branch/JAL prediction is too blunt for CoreMark when EX still owns JALR and mispredict recovery, because the extra fetch-side logic only pays off if the benchmark is dominated by backedges rather than mixed call-heavy control flow.\\n4: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.17%): Precomputing branch/JAL targets in ID only helps if the added pc+imm adder does not become part of the decode critical path, because this just moves the same control-flow cone upstream instead of shortening the end-to-end timing path.\\n5: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (regression, -8.82%): A tiny fetch-side return stack only pays off if return prediction stays aligned with the benchmark's real call/return pattern; on CoreMark, the extra stack bookkeeping and redirect recovery can outweigh the bubbles saved on JALR returns.\\n6: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n5: {\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1778914281475,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d07251c3ffeYWujJXZwFLWCpv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_e2f8dc7fe001HpkVfg8YtWZMzW\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"snapshot\":\"3c8a7a802