n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r3s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@\n+id: hyp-20260516-003-r3s2\n+title: \"Tiny BTB branch predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark has enough repeated loop branches that a purely reactive EX-stage\n+  redirect wastes fetch bandwidth on the same hot paths over and over. The\n+  prior static predictor and tiny return stack both missed too much of the real\n+  control-flow mix, so the next step is a predictor that learns actual branch\n+  behavior instead of assuming it.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a very small direct-mapped BTB/BHT in `rtl/if_stage.sv` with 2-bit\n+  saturating counters and stored targets for taken conditional branches and\n+  JAL. Keep JALR and all mispredict recovery in EX, and update the table only\n+  when a branch actually resolves. This should recover more loop iterations\n+  than the static backward predictor while keeping fetch-side logic narrow\n+  enough to avoid another Fmax regression.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a tiny BTB/BHT prediction table, use it to steer fetch on hot taken branches and JAL, and update it from resolved control-flow outcomes.\"\n","additions":22,"deletions":0}],"diagnostics":{},"truncated":false},"title":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r3s2.yaml","time":{"start":1778903336726,"end":1778903336740}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_046bc52e17035ab7016a07e9278d088198a33fb3fc7b6356fb"}},"id":"prt_e2ee6c2ec001Y9FBcN5xVlD4Lo","sessionID":"ses_1d119b854ffe1iv41ycFp5lzgs","messageID":"msg_e2ee6791b001TKMgoNyCjrS8T8"}}
