trol flow.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 306.27, \"delta_pct\": 8.29, \"vs_baseline\": 8.29, \"fmax_mhz\": 130.46, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9969, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [126.65, 130.46, 130.46], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-16T02:41:20.069705Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.29%): Static backward-branch/JAL prediction is too blunt for CoreMark when EX still owns JALR and mispredict recovery, because the extra fetch-side logic on...\n  Line 7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny return stack\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark mixes tight loops with frequent call/return traffic. The prior\\nbackward-branch predictor did not recover enough because it ignored the\\nexpensive JALR returns that dominate many short subroutine paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark mixes tight loops with frequent call/return traffic. The prior\\nbackward-branch predictor did not recover enough because it ignored the\\nexpensive JALR returns that dominate many short subroutine paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small fetch-stage return-address stack in `if_stage.sv` and use\\nit to predict JALR returns (JALR x0, x1/x5, 0) directly in fetch. Keep\\nEX-stage redirects as the recovery path for non-return JALR and any\\nmispredicts. This should remove a high-value class of control-flow bubbles\\nwith less timing risk than a broader branch predictor.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny return-address stack, push return PCs on JAL, and speculatively steer JALR-return fetches from the stack instead of waiting for EX.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": 