{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778907441892,"sessionID":"ses_1d0dbbaf3ffea553U1xJ7CeMvc","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_wujqZoP1QdCLVELMBr2eJLmY","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r4s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260516-003-r4s2\n+title: \"Tagged loop predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark still spends most of its control-flow time in a few hot loops.\n+  The prior static and broad tiny-predictor ideas paid too much in aliasing,\n+  wrong-target recovery, and fetch-side logic, which offset their IPC gains.\n+  A narrower predictor that specializes in repeated loop backedges should\n+  capture the same common-case wins with less churn.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a very small tagged loop predictor in `rtl/if_stage.sv` that learns the\n+  taken count and exit point for the hottest backward conditional branch, then\n+  speculates that loop until the learned exit. Keep JALR and all cold branches\n+  reactive in EX, and do not add a general BTB/BHT or return stack. This should\n+  recover the loop bodies CoreMark repeats most often while avoiding the aliasing\n+  and bookkeeping that hurt prior predictor attempts.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a small tagged loop predictor for backward conditional branches, learn a loop trip count from resolved outcomes, and steer fetch only for the hottest loop backedge.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r4s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r4s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs