{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778914280459,"sessionID":"ses_1d07251f5ffeB85JRWRTYUU5tY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_lPeTYwW3U0D0LFqfT7xd4pJi","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"hyp-20260516-.*predictor|predictor|branch|BTB|BHT|return stack","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments","include":"log.jsonl"},"output":"Found 9 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Gate false load-use stalls\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current hazard check treats both raw IF/ID register fields as live sources,\\neven when the decoded instruction does not actually read rs2. That can inject\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles on immediate, jump, fence, and system instructions.\\nRemoving those false stalls should raise CoreMark IPC without changing ISA behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current hazard check treats both raw IF/ID register fields as live sources,\\neven when the decoded instruction does not actually read rs2. That can inject\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles on immediate, jump, fence, and system instructions.\\nRemoving those false stalls should raise CoreMark IPC without changing ISA behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add source-usage awareness to load-use detection so only instructions that\\ntruly consume rs1/rs2 participate in the dependency compare. Keep the existing\\nstall/flush rules, but stop stalling on encodings whose rs2 bits are just\\nimmediate payload. This should recover wasted cycles in branch- and immediate-\\nheavy CoreMark code with minimal RTL risk.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass decoded instruction context into the hazard path so dependency checks can distinguish real operands from immediate fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate load-use compares with source-usage information and remove false bubbles for instructions that do not actually read rs2.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass decoded instruction context into the hazard path so dependency checks can distinguish real operands from immediate fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate load-use compares with source-usage information and remove false bubbles for instructions th...\n  Line 4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Static backward branch prediction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time in tight loops and call-heavy control flow.\\nThe current core always waits for EX to resolve taken branches, so every\\ncorrectly taken loop branch pays the full redirect bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time in tight loops and call-heavy control flow.\\nThe current core always waits for EX to resolve taken branches, so every\\ncorrectly taken loop branch pays the full redirect bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny fetch-stage predictor in `if_stage.sv` that predecodes the\\ncurrent instruction, predicts backward conditional branches and JAL as\\ntaken, and feeds the predicted target into the PC register immediately.\\nKeep EX-stage redirects as the recovery path for JALR and any mispredicts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a low-cost static predictor that computes branch/JAL targets in fetch and steers the PC before EX resolves the control flow.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a low-cost static predictor that computes branch/JAL targets in fetch and steers the PC before EX resolves the control flow.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 306.27, \"delta_pct\": 8.29, \"vs_baseline\": 8.29, \"fmax_mhz\": 130.46, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9969, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [126.65, 130.46, 130.46], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-16T02:41:20.069705Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.29%): Static backward-branch/JAL prediction is too blunt for CoreMark when EX still owns JALR and mispredict recovery, because the extra fetch-side logic on...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Precompute PC-relative targets\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The EX stage still spends timing budget on PC-relative target math even\\nthough branch and JAL destinations depend only on the already-decoded PC and\\nimmediate. Retiming that add out of EX should shorten the control-flow\\ncritical path without changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The EX stage still spends timing budget on PC-relative target math even\\nthough branch and JAL destinations depend only on the already-decoded PC and\\nimmediate. Retiming that add out of EX should shorten the control-flow\\ncritical path without changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Precompute pc+imm in ID and carry that registered target through the pipeline\\nso EX can reuse it for BEQ/BNE/BLT/BGE/JAL instead of redoing the adder on\\nthe retire-critical path. Keep JALR's rs1+imm calculation and all\\ntrap/redirect rules unchanged. This should trim the EX-stage timing cone with\\nlittle area cost and recover some Fmax on CoreMark's call/branch-heavy mix.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered PC-relative target field to the ID/EX bundle so the earlier stage can carry branch/JAL destinations forward.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the PC-relative target alongside decode and latch it with the rest of the ID/EX payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the precomputed PC-relative target for branches and JAL, leaving only JALR target formation in EX.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered PC-relative target field to the ID/EX bundle so the earlier stage can carry branch/JAL destinations forward.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the PC-relative target alongside decod...\n  Line 7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny return stack\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark mixes tight loops with frequent call/return traffic. The prior\\nbackward-branch predictor did not recover enough because it ignored the\\nexpensive JALR returns that dominate many short subroutine paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark mixes tight loops with frequent call/return traffic. The prior\\nbackward-branch predictor did not recover enough because it ignored the\\nexpensive JALR returns that dominate many short subroutine paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small fetch-stage return-address stack in `if_stage.sv` and use\\nit to predict JALR returns (JALR x0, x1/x5, 0) directly in fetch. Keep\\nEX-stage redirects as the recovery path for non-return JALR and any\\nmispredicts. This should remove a high-value class of control-flow bubbles\\nwith less timing risk than a broader branch predictor.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny return-address stack, push return PCs on JAL, and speculatively steer JALR-return fetches from the stack instead of waiting for EX.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny return-address stack, push return PCs on JAL, and speculatively steer JALR-return fetches from the stack instead of waiting for EX.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 283.5, \"delta_pct\": -8.82, \"vs_baseline\": 0.24, \"fmax_mhz\": 140.02, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4938989, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10613, \"ff\": 2124, \"seeds\": [140.02, 139.57, 151.91], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-16T03:47:21.033640Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (regression, -8.82%): A tiny fetch-side return stack only pays off if return prediction stays ...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Early branch resolve\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark burns a lot of cycles on tight conditional loops, and this core still\\nwaits until EX to confirm taken branches. That leaves a full redirect bubble on\\nthe hot path even though the branch operands are already available much earlier\\nin the pipeline.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark burns a lot of cycles on tight conditional loops, and this core still\\nwaits until EX to confirm taken branches. That leaves a full redirect bubble on\\nthe hot path even though the branch operands are already available much earlier\\nin the pipeline.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move exact resolution of BEQ/BNE/BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU into ID with a dedicated\\ncompare-and-target path that uses the decoded operands before EX runs. Keep\\nJALR and all mispredict recovery in EX, and leave the ALU and memory paths\\nuntouched. This should cut one cycle from the common taken-branch case on\\nCoreMark's loop-heavy control flow without adding speculative predictor state.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Provide branch-facing operand selection so the ID stage can compare the freshest rs1/rs2 values before EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Resolve conditional branches early and emit the exact redirect target for taken cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Route the ID-stage redirect into fetch and keep EX as the fallback path for JALR and recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain JAL/JALR and trap handling, but stop re-resolving conditional branches that ID already handled.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Provide branch-facing operand selection so the ID stage can compare the freshest rs1/rs2 values before EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"desc...\n  Line 10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny BTB branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark has enough repeated loop branches that a purely reactive EX-stage\\nredirect wastes fetch bandwidth on the same hot paths over and over. The\\nprior static predictor and tiny return stack both missed too much of the real\\ncontrol-flow mix, so the next step is a predictor that learns actual branch\\nbehavior instead of assuming it.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark has enough repeated loop branches that a purely reactive EX-stage\\nredirect wastes fetch bandwidth on the same hot paths over and over. The\\nprior static predictor and tiny return stack both missed too much of the real\\ncontrol-flow mix, so the next step is a predictor that learns actual branch\\nbehavior instead of assuming it.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped BTB/BHT in `rtl/if_stage.sv` with 2-bit\\nsaturating counters and stored targets for taken conditional branches and\\nJAL. Keep JALR and all mispredict recovery in EX, and update the table only\\nwhen a branch actually resolves. This should recover more loop iterations\\nthan the static backward predictor while keeping fetch-side logic narrow\\nenough to avoid another Fmax regression.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny BTB/BHT prediction table, use it to steer fetch on hot taken branches and JAL, and update it from resolved control-flow outcomes.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny BTB/BHT prediction table, use it to steer fetch on hot taken branches and JAL, and update it from resolved control-flow outcomes.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 308.34, \"delta_pct\": -0.84, \"vs_baseline\": 9.02, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.91, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4407728, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10799, \"ff\": 2518, \"seeds\":...\n  Line 13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Tagged loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends most of its control-flow time in a few hot loops.\\nThe prior static and broad tiny-predictor ideas paid too much in aliasing,\\nwrong-target recovery, and fetch-side logic, which offset their IPC gains.\\nA narrower predictor that specializes in repeated loop backedges should\\ncapture the same common-case wins with less churn.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends most of its control-flow time in a few hot loops.\\nThe prior static and broad tiny-predictor ideas paid too much in aliasing,\\nwrong-target recovery, and fetch-side logic, which offset their IPC gains.\\nA narrower predictor that specializes in repeated loop backedges should\\ncapture the same common-case wins with less churn.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small tagged loop predictor in `rtl/if_stage.sv` that learns the\\ntaken count and exit point for the hottest backward conditional branch, then\\nspeculates that loop until the learned exit. Keep JALR and all cold branches\\nreactive in EX, and do not add a general BTB/BHT or return stack. This should\\nrecover the loop bodies CoreMark repeats most often while avoiding the aliasing\\nand bookkeeping that hurt prior predictor attempts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small tagged loop predictor for backward conditional branches, learn a loop trip count from resolved outcomes, and steer fetch only for the hottest loop backedge.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small tagged loop predictor for backward conditional branches, learn a loop trip count from resolved outcomes, and steer fetch only for the hottest loop backedge.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 310.94, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 9.94, \"fmax_mhz\": 15...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed forwarding cut\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The execute stage currently pays for operand compare plus operand muxing on\\nevery instruction before it can even reach the ALU or branch compare. That\\nkeeps a wide, high-fanout forwarding cone on the retire-critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The execute stage currently pays for operand compare plus operand muxing on\\nevery instruction before it can even reach the ALU or branch compare. That\\nkeeps a wide, high-fanout forwarding cone on the retire-critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move final rs1/rs2 forwarding selection into ID so the ID/EX register latches\\nsettled execute operands, and let EX consume those values directly. This keeps\\nretirement and hazard behavior unchanged while shortening the execute-stage\\ncombinational path and reducing fanout from EX/MEM and MEM/WB into the ALU.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Route forwarding selects and source values into ID instead of EX, while keeping the existing hazard and RVFI plumbing intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch already-forwarded rs1/rs2 operands into the ID/EX register so downstream logic sees settled execute inputs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Drop the per-operand forwarding muxes and consume the latched operands directly in the ALU and branch logic.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Route forwarding selects and source values into ID instead of EX, while keeping the existing hazard and RVFI plumbing intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch already-forwarded rs1/rs2 operands into the ID/EX register so downstream logic sees settled execute inputs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Drop the per-operand forwarding muxes and consume t...\n  Line 16: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r5s2.yaml\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"schema_error: while scanning a simple key\\n  in \\\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r5s2.yaml\\\", line 7, column 1\\ncould not find expected ':'\\n  in \\\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r5s2.yaml\\\", line 8, column 1\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 5}\n","metadata":{"matches":9,"truncated":false},"title":"hyp-20260516-.*predictor|predictor|branch|BTB|BHT|return stack","time":{"start":1778914280405,"end":1778914280456}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0f592ca7f26353fb016a0813e81604819b92b599eda10ce08d"}},"id":"prt_e2f8dc3c4001emUHrKNZod71ie","sessionID":"ses_1d07251f5ffeB85JRWRTYUU5tY","messageID":"msg_e2f8dafdf001hmcfk5QDkZegAQ"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778914280483,"sessionID":"ses_1d07251f5ffeB85JRWRTYUU5tY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_h7WGGPVvcvqyD9jvEdxYJGpK","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md","offset":1,"limit":400},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Load-use interlocks should gate on decoder-derived rs1/rs2 usage bits, because raw register-field compares can falsely stall immediates, jumps, fences, and system ops on payload-only operand bits.\n2: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +9.94%): Turning IF into a real registered IF/ID cutpoint raised Fmax by removing the live io_imemData-to-decode combinational chain, but the register has to own flush/redirect/bubble behavior so control-flow changes still land as NOPs.\n3: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.29%): Static backward-branch/JAL prediction is too blunt for CoreMark when EX still owns JALR and mispredict recovery, because the extra fetch-side logic only pays off if the benchmark is dominated by backedges rather than mixed call-heavy control flow.\n4: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.17%): Precomputing branch/JAL targets in ID only helps if the added pc+imm adder does not become part of the decode critical path, because this just moves the same control-flow cone upstream instead of shortening the end-to-end timing path.\n5: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (regression, -8.82%): A tiny fetch-side return stack only pays off if return prediction stays aligned with the benchmark's real call/return pattern; on CoreMark, the extra stack bookkeeping and redirect recovery can outweigh the bubbles saved on JALR returns.\n6: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): Early branch resolution in ID must reuse the same forwarding and stall discipline as EX, or stale compare operands will misdirect the PC and trip the formal pc_fwd/reg checks.\n7: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r3s2 (regression, -0.84%): A learned predictor only beats the static backward guess if the BTB/BHT is large enough for CoreMark's hot branch footprint; a tiny direct-mapped table just adds aliasing and wrong-target churn to an already loop-heavy control flow.\n8: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r4s0 (regression, -14.58%): Manually replacing a variable load-data shift with a byte/halfword lane-select tree can hurt Fmax if the original shift was not the true critical path, because the extra muxing and decode fanout outweigh the hoped-for cone reduction.\n9: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r4s1 (regression, -9.36%): Predecoding instruction fields in IF only helps if the fetch-side stage has slack to absorb the decoder and imm-gen cone; otherwise the work just moves the timing hotspot upstream and increases instruction-bit fanout without shortening the critical path.\n10: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r5s1 (broken, n/a): Moving bypass selection into ID only works if you keep the forwarded execute operands distinct from the raw regfile readbacks RVFI records; otherwise the core can retire with stale source-operand metadata even when the data path itself is functionally close.\n\n(End of file - total 10 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Load-use interlocks should gate on decoder-derived rs1/rs2 usage bits, because raw register-field compares can falsely stall immediates, jumps, fences, and system ops on payload-only operand bits.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +9.94%): Turning IF into a real registered IF/ID cutpoint raised Fmax by removing the live io_imemData-to-decode combinational chain, but the register has to own flush/redirect/bubble behavior so control-flow changes still land as NOPs.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.29%): Static backward-branch/JAL prediction is too blunt for CoreMark when EX still owns JALR and mispredict recovery, because the extra fetch-side logic only pays off if the benchmark is dominated by backedges rather than mixed call-heavy control flow.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.17%): Precomputing branch/JAL targets in ID only helps if the added pc+imm adder does not become part of the decode critical path, because this just moves the same control-flow cone upstream instead of shortening the end-to-end timing path.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (regression, -8.82%): A tiny fetch-side return stack only pays off if return prediction stays aligned with the benchmark's real call/return pattern; on CoreMark, the extra stack bookkeeping and redirect recovery can outweigh the bubbles saved on JALR returns.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): Early branch resolution in ID must reuse the same forwarding and stall discipline as EX, or stale compare operands will misdirect the PC and trip the formal pc_fwd/reg checks.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r3s2 (regression, -0.84%): A learned predictor only beats the static backward guess if the BTB/BHT is large enough for CoreMark's hot branch footprint; a tiny direct-mapped table just adds aliasing and wrong-target churn to an already loop-heavy control flow.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r4s0 (regression, -14.58%): Manually replacing a variable load-data shift with a byte/halfword lane-select tree can hurt Fmax if the original shift was not the true critical path, because the extra muxing and decode fanout outweigh the hoped-for cone reduction.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r4s1 (regression, -9.36%): Predecoding instruction fields in IF only helps if the fetch-side stage has slack to absorb the decoder and imm-gen cone; otherwise the work just moves the timing hotspot upstream and increases instruction-bit fanout without shortening the critical path.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r5s1 (broken, n/a): Moving bypass selection into ID only works if you keep the forwarded execute operands distinct from the raw regfile readbacks RVFI records; otherwise the core can retire with stale source-operand metadata even when the data path itself is functionally close.","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md","time":{"start":1778914280384,"end":1778914280481}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0f592ca7f26353fb016a0813e815f0819bb4ee26be8c5cdf25"}},"id":"prt_e2f8dc351001wjBaaNFUvrbIc4","sessionID":"ses_1d07251f5ffeB85JRWRTYUU5tY","messageID":"msg_e2f8dafdf001hmcfk5QDkZegAQ"}}
