{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778934571987,"sessionID":"ses_1cf3dfab1ffebLwYgexkhWsZ55","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_ZwiDoDRHEYACsSvMsWmC6nro","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r9s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260516-003-r9s2\n+title: \"Direct-call target cache\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark is not only loop-heavy; it also revisits the same direct call sites\n+  many times. The previous branch-, return-, and loop-oriented predictors lost\n+  ground to aliasing, bookkeeping, or Fmax pressure because they tried to\n+  cover too much control flow at once.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a tiny direct-call target cache in `rtl/if_stage.sv` that records the\n+  PC and resolved target of hot JAL call sites, then steers fetch to the cached\n+  target on a later hit. Leave conditional branches, JALR returns, and all\n+  mispredict recovery in EX. Narrowing speculation to direct calls should\n+  recover repeated CoreMark subroutine-entry bubbles without the wider BTB/RAS\n+  state that hurt earlier predictor attempts.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a small call-target cache keyed by fetch PC, learn JAL targets from resolved redirects, and use cache hits to redirect fetch early.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r9s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r9s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r9s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/