it to steer fetch on the hottest loop backedges.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: insn_beq_ch0\\n1 (11)\\nSBY  1:15:12 [insn_xor_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:11 (11)\\nSBY  1:15:12 [insn_xor_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\nSBY  1:15:12 [insn_xor_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\\nSBY  1:15:12 [insn_xor_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\nSBY  1:15:1...\n  Line 32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Usage-gated compare cone\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current hazard and forwarding paths still compare raw rs1/rs2 fields even\\nwhen the younger instruction does not actually read those operands. That leaves\\ndead compare fanout on a hot control path and can also inject false load-use\\nstalls on immediate, jump, fence, and SYSTEM encodings.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current hazard and forwarding paths still compare raw rs1/rs2 fields even\\nwhen the younger instruction does not actually read those operands. That leaves\\ndead compare fanout on a hot control path and can also inject false load-use\\nstalls on immediate, jump, fence, and SYSTEM encodings.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add decoder-derived rs1/rs2 usage bits and thread them through the pipeline so\\nthe load-use interlock and forwarding compares only fire for real operand reads.\\nKeep operand data, RVFI snapshots, and retirement ordering unchanged; only the\\ndead compare cone and payload-only false stalls should disappear. This should\\nrecover a few CoreMark cycles while also shortening the stall/forwarding path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add rs1/rs2 usage sideband bits to the pipeline bundles so decode can carry whether each source is live.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Classify which instruction forms actually read rs1 and rs2 alongside the existing control outputs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the usage bits with the decoded instruction so downstream logic can consume them without re-decoding.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the decoded source-usage sideband into the hazard and forwarding paths while leaving RVFI operand data unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate load-use compares with source-usage bits so payload-only register fields do not stall the pipe.\"},...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Backward-branch-only predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in tight loop backedges, but the\\nbroader predictor attempts lost ground to fetch-side bookkeeping, aliasing,\\nand Fmax pressure. A much narrower predictor that only helps the hot\\nbackward conditional-branch case should recover useful bubbles without\\ncarrying the extra state that hurt the earlier BTB, RAS, and loop variants.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in tight loop backedges, but the\\nbroader predictor attempts lost ground to fetch-side bookkeeping, aliasing,\\nand Fmax pressure. A much narrower predictor that only helps the hot\\nbackward conditional-branch case should recover useful bubbles without\\ncarrying the extra state that hurt the earlier BTB, RAS, and loop variants.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a branch-only fetch predictor in `rtl/if_stage.sv` that predicts\\nbackward conditional branches as taken and steers fetch directly to the\\ncomputed branch target. Do not speculate JAL/JALR, do not add a BTB/BHT or\\nreturn stack, and keep EX-stage redirect as the recovery path for all\\nmisses. This keeps the predictor logic small while targeting the loop\\nbackedges CoreMark repeats most often.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a minimal backward-conditional-branch predictor that computes the B-type target in fetch and redirects only for predicted-taken loop backedges.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a minimal backward-conditional-branch predictor that computes the B-type target in fetch and redirects only for predicted-taken loop backedges.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 394.64, \"delta_pct\": 10.5, \"vs_baseline\": 39.54, \"fmax_mhz\": 186.22, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 471...\n  Line 35: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-001-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Usage-gated hazard cone\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The hot hazard and forwarding paths still compare raw rs1/rs2 fields even when\\nthe younger instruction does not actually read them. That leaves dead compare\\nfanout on the stall path and can also inject false load-use bubbles on payload-\\nonly encodings, which is wasted work on CoreMark's control-heavy mix.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The hot hazard and forwarding paths still compare raw rs1/rs2 fields even when\\nthe younger instruction does not actually read them. That leaves dead compare\\nfanout on the stall path and can also inject false load-use bubbles on payload-\\nonly encodings, which is wasted work on CoreMark's control-heavy mix.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add decoder-derived rs1/rs2 usage bits and carry them through the pipeline so\\nload-use detection and operand forwarding only fire for real source reads.\\nKeep operand data, RVFI snapshots, and retirement order unchanged; the only\\narchitectural effect should be fewer false stalls and a shorter compare cone\\non the PC-hold / bypass paths.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add rs1/rs2 usage sideband bits to the pipeline bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Classify which decoded instruction forms actually read rs1 and rs2.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the usage bits with the decoded instruction bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the usage sideband into the hazard and forwarding paths.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate load-use compares with source-usage bits so payload-only fields do not stall the pipe.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Skip dead operand compares for instructions that do not consume a source register.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"fil...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Pipelined redirect recovery\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The backward-branch predictor already covers the hot loop path, so the\\nremaining timing pressure is more likely coming from the same-cycle EX-to-IF\\nrecovery wire than from common-case fetch. Cutting that redirect path should\\nshorten the longest control-flow cone without changing the architectural\\nretirement model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The backward-branch predictor already covers the hot loop path, so the\\nremaining timing pressure is more likely coming from the same-cycle EX-to-IF\\nrecovery wire than from common-case fetch. Cutting that redirect path should\\nshorten the longest control-flow cone without changing the architectural\\nretirement model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Register the resolved redirect from EX before IF consumes it, so mispredict\\nrecovery becomes a one-cycle recovery path instead of a same-cycle\\ncombinational override. Keep the predicted-taken fast path unchanged and\\npreserve the exact architectural next PC in EX/RVFI; only the rare recovery\\ncase should pay the extra cycle. This should improve Fmax by removing the\\nlong EX-to-fetch feedback path that still spans branch resolution, target\\nselection, and PC update.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Insert a registered redirect sideband between EX and IF so fetch consumes a settled recovery request instead of a live combinational one.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Produce the architecturally resolved redirect target as a latched request/target pair without changing branch, jump, or trap semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Prioritize the registered recovery request over the predictor when selecting the next PC, while leaving the predicted-taken fast path intact.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [...\n  Line 37: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-003-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Two-entry backward predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends most of its control-flow budget on a small set of\\nrepeated loop backedges. The last successful hypothesis showed that a very\\nnarrow backward-branch predictor can improve fitness without dragging in the\\nBTB, RAS, or JAL/JALR bookkeeping that hurt broader predictor attempts.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends most of its control-flow budget on a small set of\\nrepeated loop backedges. The last successful hypothesis showed that a very\\nnarrow backward-branch predictor can improve fitness without dragging in the\\nBTB, RAS, or JAL/JALR bookkeeping that hurt broader predictor attempts.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny two-entry tagged predictor in `rtl/if_stage.sv` that only learns\\nbackward conditional branches. Each entry should remember the branch PC,\\ntarget, and a small taken-confidence counter so fetch can redirect early on\\nthe hottest loop backedges while cold branches still fall back to the current\\nEX-stage recovery path. Keep JAL, JALR, and forward branches reactive so the\\npredictor stays narrow and cheap to time.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny two-entry tagged predictor for backward conditional branches, with learned target/confidence state and EX redirect recovery for misses.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny two-entry tagged predictor for backward conditional branches, with learned target/confidence state and EX redirect recovery for misses.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 141.87, \"delta_pct\": -64.05, \"vs_baseline\": -49.84, \"fmax_mhz\": 67.06, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4727002, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10577, \"ff\": 2322, \"seeds\": [67.06, 62.09, 77.25], \"formal_passed\": true, \"c...\n  Line 38: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-001-r13s0\", \"title\": \"Usage-gated compare cone\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The core still spends timing budget comparing raw rs1/rs2 fields even when\\nthe younger instruction does not actually read one of them. That leaves dead\\ncompare fanout on the hazard and forwarding paths and can also inject false\\nload-use stalls on payload-only encodings, which is wasted work on CoreMark's\\ncontrol-heavy mix.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The core still spends timing budget comparing raw rs1/rs2 fields even when\\nthe younger instruction does not actually read one of them. That leaves dead\\ncompare fanout on the hazard and forwarding paths and can also inject false\\nload-use stalls on payload-only encodings, which is wasted work on CoreMark's\\ncontrol-heavy mix.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add decoder-derived rs1/rs2 usage bits and carry them through the pipeline so\\nload-use detection and operand forwarding only fire for real source reads.\\nKeep operand data, RVFI snapshots, and retirement order unchanged; the only\\narchitectural effect should be fewer false stalls and a shorter compare cone\\non the PC-hold and bypass paths. This should recover a few CoreMark cycles\\nwhile also trimming the hot hazard logic.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add rs1/rs2 usage sideband bits to the pipeline bundles so decode can carry whether each source is live.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Classify which decoded instruction forms actually read rs1 and rs2 alongside the existing control outputs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the usage bits with the decoded instruction so downstream logic can consume them without re-decoding.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the decoded source-usage sideband into the hazard and forwarding paths while leaving RVFI operand data unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\"...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Hoist immediates out of ID\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"ID still rebuilds immediates on every instruction even though the value is\\nonly a function of the fetched word. Moving that bit-slice out of the decode\\ncone should shorten the common ID path without changing the pipeline contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"ID still rebuilds immediates on every instruction even though the value is\\nonly a function of the fetched word. Moving that bit-slice out of the decode\\ncone should shorten the common ID path without changing the pipeline contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a precomputed immediate field to the IF/ID bundle, generate it from the\\nregistered instruction in fetch, and have ID consume that latched value\\ninstead of instantiating `imm_gen` locally. Keep stall, flush, redirect, and\\nRVFI behavior unchanged; this is only a structural split of the immediate\\nconstruction logic so decode can spend less time on the critical path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an immediate field to the IF/ID bundle so fetch can carry the precomputed value forward.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the instruction immediate after the fetch register and latch it with the IF/ID payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the precomputed immediate from the bundle and drop the local `imm_gen` instance.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an immediate field to the IF/ID bundle so fetch can carry the precomputed value forward.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the instruction immediate after the fetch register and latch it with the IF/ID payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the precomputed immediate from the bundle and drop the local `imm_gen` i...\n  Line 40: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-003-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Cheaper backward predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current backward-branch predictor already proved that CoreMark benefits\\nfrom speculating loop backedges, but the fetch-side cone still pays for the\\npredictor's target math on every cycle. A narrower implementation that keeps\\nthe same prediction policy while reducing common-path logic could raise Fmax\\nwithout giving up the IPC win.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current backward-branch predictor already proved that CoreMark benefits\\nfrom speculating loop backedges, but the fetch-side cone still pays for the\\npredictor's target math on every cycle. A narrower implementation that keeps\\nthe same prediction policy while reducing common-path logic could raise Fmax\\nwithout giving up the IPC win.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the existing backward-conditional-branch prediction policy, but split\\nthe decision from target formation so fetch first classifies the instruction\\nas a backward branch and only then computes the taken target. Do not add BTB,\\nRAS, JAL/JALR speculation, or confidence state; just make the hot not-taken\\npath cheaper while preserving EX-stage redirect recovery. This should trim\\npredictor fanout in IF and retain the loop-backedge coverage that worked\\nbest so far.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor the backward-branch predictor so the taken/not-taken decision is made before target formation, reducing fetch-side combinational cost while keeping the same recovery behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor the backward-branch predictor so the taken/not-taken decision is made before target formation, reducing fetch-side combinational cost while keeping the same recovery behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 354.37, \"de...\n  Line 41: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-001-r14s0\", \"title\": \"Gate control-flow cone\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The EX stage currently evaluates branch compare and target math on every\\ninstruction, even though only control-flow ops need that logic. Keeping the\\ncompare/add cone live for non-branches wastes timing budget on the common\\nALU path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The EX stage currently evaluates branch compare and target math on every\\ninstruction, even though only control-flow ops need that logic. Keeping the\\ncompare/add cone live for non-branches wastes timing budget on the common\\nALU path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Guard the branch-condition compare and PC-relative target math in\\n`ex_stage.sv` so they only activate for actual branch or jump instructions.\\nKeep JALR, misalign trapping, redirect recovery, and RVFI semantics\\nunchanged; the only effect should be that ordinary ALU and memory ops stop\\npaying for the always-on control-flow cone. This should trim the hot EX path\\nenough to improve Fmax without changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Condition branch comparison and target formation on control-flow opcodes so non-branch instructions do not carry the full control-flow cone.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Condition branch comparison and target formation on control-flow opcodes so non-branch instructions do not carry the full control-flow cone.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 364.12, \"delta_pct\": -7.73, \"vs_baseline\": 28.75, \"fmax_mhz\": 171.82, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4718765, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10598, \"ff\": 2137, \"seeds\": [171.82, 180.38, 170.97], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-17T06:57:30.761619Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 14, \"les...\n  Line 43: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-003-r14s2\", \"title\": \"Predict JALs in fetch\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark mixes hot loop backedges with frequent calls, but the current fetch predictor only helps backward conditional branches. That leaves every unconditional JAL to pay a full EX redirect penalty even though its target is known from the instruction bits.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark mixes hot loop backedges with frequent calls, but the current fetch predictor only helps backward conditional branches. That leaves every unconditional JAL to pay a full EX redirect penalty even though its target is known from the instruction bits.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the existing IF-side predictor to treat JAL as always taken, using a small J-type immediate decode and the same redirect path already used for backward branches. Keep JALR and all architecturally resolved recovery in EX so the change stays table-free and avoids the bookkeeping that hurt prior BTB/RAS attempts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add fetch-side JAL prediction alongside the existing backward-branch predictor, with no BTB, RAS, or confidence table.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add fetch-side JAL prediction alongside the existing backward-branch predictor, with no BTB, RAS, or confidence table.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 394.64, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 39.54, \"fmax_mhz\": 186.22, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4718765, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10286, \"ff\": 2137, \"seeds\": [177.3, 186.22, 191.42], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-17T07:05:33.422967Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 14, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-17 hyp-20260517-003-r14s2 (regression, +0.00%): Predicting JALs in fetch only pays off when the J-t...","metadata":{"matches":40,"truncated":false},"title":"micro_opt|forward_unit|id_stage|hazard_unit|if_stage|mem_stage","time":{"start":1779001591384,"end":1779001591409}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_05439c12f2797286016a0968f7411c819098945a8ee7117bee"}},"id":"prt_e34c20654001nI2CM810GJ82AV","sessionID":"ses_1cb3e19eeffeUdOgAsl15dsfjk","messageID":"msg_e34c1e6fa001DFVIzPeT9YVF3q"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1779001591416,"sessionID":"ses_1cb3e19eeffeUdOgAsl15dsfjk","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_mWvPnw6zT6CIhixYU8L1qQz6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md","offset":1,"limit":400},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Load-use interlocks should gate on decoder-derived rs1/rs2 usage bits, because raw register-field compares can falsely stall immediates, jumps, fences, and system ops on payload-only operand bits.\n2: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +9.94%): Turning IF into a real registered IF/ID cutpoint raised Fmax by removing the live io_imemData-to-decode combinational chain, but the register has to own flush/redirect/bubble behavior so control-flow changes still land as NOPs.\n3: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.29%): Static backward-branch/JAL prediction is too blunt for CoreMark when EX still owns JALR and mispredict recovery, because the extra fetch-side logic only pays off if the benchmark is dominated by backedges rather than mixed call-heavy control flow.\n4: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.17%): Precomputing branch/JAL targets in ID only helps if the added pc+imm adder does not become part of the decode critical path, because this just moves the same control-flow cone upstream instead of shortening the end-to-end timing path.\n5: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (regression, -8.82%): A tiny fetch-side return stack only pays off if return prediction stays aligned with the benchmark's real call/return pattern; on CoreMark, the extra stack bookkeeping and redirect recovery can outweigh the bubbles saved on JALR returns.\n6: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): Early branch resolution in ID must reuse the same forwarding and stall discipline as EX, or stale compare operands will misdirect the PC and trip the formal pc_fwd/reg checks.\n7: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r3s2 (regression, -0.84%): A learned predictor only beats the static backward guess if the BTB/BHT is large enough for CoreMark's hot branch footprint; a tiny direct-mapped table just adds aliasing and wrong-target churn to an already loop-heavy control flow.\n8: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r4s0 (regression, -14.58%): Manually replacing a variable load-data shift with a byte/halfword lane-select tree can hurt Fmax if the original shift was not the true critical path, because the extra muxing and decode fanout outweigh the hoped-for cone reduction.\n9: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r4s1 (regression, -9.36%): Predecoding instruction fields in IF only helps if the fetch-side stage has slack to absorb the decoder and imm-gen cone; otherwise the work just moves the timing hotspot upstream and increases instruction-bit fanout without shortening the critical path.\n10: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r5s1 (broken, n/a): Moving bypass selection into ID only works if you keep the forwarded execute operands distinct from the raw regfile readbacks RVFI records; otherwise the core can retire with stale source-operand metadata even when the data path itself is functionally close.\n11: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r6s2 (broken, n/a): A tiny tagged 2-way BTB/BHT only helps if the extra tag/way/LRU state still fits the formal timeout budget; once predictor bookkeeping becomes the heaviest part of the fetch cone, `run_all.sh` can stall before any CoreMark benefit is measurable.\n12: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r7s1 (regression, -16.15%): Retiming bypass muxing into ID can backfire if the added compare and forwarding fanout on decode is heavier than the ALU cone it removes, because the critical path just shifts upstream instead of getting shorter.\n13: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r8s2 (regression, -28.96%): A one-entry learned loop predictor is still too narrow for CoreMark if it has to track only one hot backedge at a time, because the compare/update plumbing and EX recovery cost can outweigh the hits when multiple backward branches share the fetch stream.\n14: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r9s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-derived `rs1_used`/`rs2_used` bits can trim hazard compares only if the RVFI snapshot path mirrors the same forwarding decisions; a separate always-forward snapshot mux tree is easy to desync and will trip `rvfi_reg_check`.\n15: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r9s1 (improvement, +14.86%): Pulling MUL/DIV/REM onto a separate registered backend improved timing because the frequent RV32I path no longer shared the multiplier/divider cone, but the design had to stall only while an M op was actually in flight so common ADD/SUB/branch work stayed single-cycle.\n16: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r9s2 (regression, -11.00%): A tiny direct-call cache still loses if its PC/tag compare, update, and recovery plumbing sit in IF, because CoreMark's repeated JAL call sites are not hot enough to repay the extra fetch-side fanout and aliasing.\n17: - 2026-05-17 hyp-20260517-003-r10s2 (broken, n/a): A loop predictor can only bypass the normal redirect path if EX still publishes the architecturally resolved next PC for every conditional branch; otherwise the branch and `pc_fwd` formal checks will catch a fetch/resolve contract break.\n18: - 2026-05-17 hyp-20260517-002-r11s1 (broken, n/a): When splitting DIV/REM into a separate iterative backend, every shift/remainder temporary must be driven on all `always_comb` paths or synthesis will infer latches and erase the timing win before the new handshake can help.\n19: - 2026-05-17 hyp-20260517-003-r11s2 (improvement, +10.50%): Restricting fetch prediction to backward conditional branches hit CoreMark's hot loop backedges without the BTB/RAS/JALR bookkeeping that had been hurting Fmax, so a tiny `pred_taken` bit plus EX mispredict recovery was enough to win.\n20: - 2026-05-17 hyp-20260517-002-r12s1 (regression, -4.98%): Registering EX redirect recovery can backfire if the branch predictor already covers the hot backedge path, because the extra one-cycle fix-up only slows the rare mispredict/jump recovery without shortening the common fetch cone.\n21: - 2026-05-17 hyp-20260517-003-r12s2 (regression, -64.05%): A confidence-gated two-entry backedge predictor needs enough hit-rate gain to pay for the extra PC/target compare and update cone in IF; on CoreMark, that bookkeeping outweighed the few bubbles it could save.\n22: - 2026-05-17 hyp-20260517-003-r13s2 (regression, -10.20%): Splitting backward-branch classification from target formation did not rescue Fmax here, which means the backward-taken policy only helps CoreMark when the fetch cone actually wins back enough logic to offset the extra gating and redirect recovery.\n23: - 2026-05-17 hyp-20260517-001-r14s0 (regression, -7.73%): Gating branch compare and target math behind `is_branch`/`is_jump` only helps if that cone is truly on the EX critical path; on this CoreMark run the extra control gating did not buy back enough timing to offset the added decode/condition plumbing.\n24: - 2026-05-17 hyp-20260517-003-r14s2 (regression, +0.00%): Predicting JALs in fetch only pays off when the J-type target decode and redirect muxing stay cheap enough to beat the cost of extra IF-side control flow logic; on CoreMark, the call fast path was not hot enough to repay that added fetch cone.\n\n(End of file - total 24 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Load-use interlocks should gate on decoder-derived rs1/rs2 usage bits, because raw register-field compares can falsely stall immediates, jumps, fences, and system ops on payload-only operand bits.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +9.94%): Turning IF into a real registered IF/ID cutpoint raised Fmax by removing the live io_imemData-to-decode combinational chain, but the register has to own flush/redirect/bubble behavior so control-flow changes still land as NOPs.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.29%): Static backward-branch/JAL prediction is too blunt for CoreMark when EX still owns JALR and mispredict recovery, because the extra fetch-side logic only pays off if the benchmark is dominated by backedges rather than mixed call-heavy control flow.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.17%): Precomputing branch/JAL targets in ID only helps if the added pc+imm adder does not become part of the decode critical path, because this just moves the same control-flow cone upstream instead of shortening the end-to-end timing path.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (regression, -8.82%): A tiny fetch-side return stack only pays off if return prediction stays aligned with the benchmark's real call/return pattern; on CoreMark, the extra stack bookkeeping and redirect recovery can outweigh the bubbles saved on JALR returns.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): Early branch resolution in ID must reuse the same forwarding and stall discipline as EX, or stale compare operands will misdirect the PC and trip the formal pc_fwd/reg checks.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r3s2 (regression, -0.84%): A learned predictor only beats the static backward guess if the BTB/BHT is large enough for CoreMark's hot branch footprint; a tiny direct-mapped table just adds aliasing and wrong-target churn to an already loop-heavy control flow.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r4s0 (regression, -14.58%): Manually replacing a variable load-data shift with a byte/halfword lane-select tree can hurt Fmax if the original shift was not the true critical path, because the extra muxing and decode fanout outweigh the hoped-for cone reduction.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r4s1 (regression, -9.36%): Predecoding instruction fields in IF only helps if the fetch-side stage has slack to absorb the decoder and imm-gen cone; otherwise the work just moves the timing hotspot upstream and increases instruction-bit fanout without shortening the critical path.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r5s1 (broken, n/a): Moving bypass selection into ID only works if you keep the forwarded execute operands distinct from the raw regfile readbacks RVFI records; otherwise the core can retire with stale source-operand metadata even when the data path itself is functionally close.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r6s2 (broken, n/a): A tiny tagged 2-way BTB/BHT only helps if the extra tag/way/LRU state still fits the formal timeout budget; once predictor bookkeeping becomes the heaviest part of the fetch cone, `run_all.sh` can stall before any CoreMark benefit is measurable.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r7s1 (regression, -16.15%): Retiming bypass muxing into ID can backfire if the added compare and forwarding fanout on decode is heavier than the ALU cone it removes, because the critical path just shifts upstream instead of getting shorter.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r8s2 (regression, -28.96%): A one-entry learned loop predictor is still too narrow for CoreMark if it has to track only one hot backedge at a time, because the compare/update plumbing and EX recovery cost can outweigh the hits when multiple backward branches share the fetch stream.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r9s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-derived `rs1_used`/`rs2_used` bits can trim hazard compares only if the RVFI snapshot path mirrors the same forwarding decisions; a separate always-forward snapshot mux tree is easy to desync and will trip `rvfi_reg_check`.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r9s1 (improvement, +14.86%): Pulling MUL/DIV/REM onto a separate registered backend improved timing because the frequent RV32I path no longer shared the multiplier/divider cone, but the design had to stall only while an M op was actually in flight so common ADD/SUB/branch work stayed single-cycle.\n- 2026-0