{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778991517221,"sessionID":"ses_1cbd98ff6ffehmlWSYIW1c1qoi","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_87Q2dxVBfpj9iQOF0o85iuDn","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-002-r11s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260517-002-r11s1\n+title: \"Split divider from M backend\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The prior M-path split helped because the frequent RV32I path stopped sharing\n+  the multiplier/divider cone. The remaining divider work is still the widest\n+  arithmetic block in the core, so it is the next clean place to cut timing\n+  without disturbing decode, forwarding, or memory behavior.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Keep MUL on the fast path, but move DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a tiny iterative\n+  divider with registered state and the existing start/busy/done handshake.\n+  That removes the wide divide cone from the synthesized combinational logic\n+  while preserving the current architectural stall model for rare M-ops. The\n+  common RV32I/CoreMark path should close timing more easily, and the extra\n+  cycles on divide/remainder should be amortized by their low frequency.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/m_backend.sv\n+    description: \"Split the registered M backend so MUL remains fast while DIV/REM use a tiny iterative state machine instead of a wide combinational divide cone.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-002-r11s1.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-002-r11s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.clau