{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1779000132945,"sessionID":"ses_1cb55a9efffeKVZPw3kPHWAkv8","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_A8GaYbYxH2lBvB7wge82BAOk","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-002-r14s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260517-002-r14s1\n+title: \"Drop regfile bypass\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The regfile still spends combinational work on same-cycle write-first\n+  bypassing for both read ports, even though the pipeline already has EX/MEM\n+  and MEM/WB forwarding for real RAW hazards. Removing that read-port mux\n+  cone should shorten ID without changing architected results.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Remove the regfile's same-cycle write-first bypass and make reads return\n+  only the stored array contents, while keeping x0 hardwired to zero and the\n+  existing EX/MEM + MEM/WB forwarding network intact. That should trim the\n+  hot compare/mux path on every decode cycle and let the later forwarding\n+  logic handle recency when consumers reach EX.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n+    description: \"Delete the write-first read-port bypass so regfile reads become plain array lookups with x0 still forced to zero.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-002-r14s1.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-002-r14s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-002-r14s1.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-002-r14s1.ya