{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778990313615,"sessionID":"ses_1cbeac6beffeqjJF69SouT2N3T","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_gU0zngRfQ49h5hiGjMDucVlM","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-003-r10s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260517-003-r10s2\n+title: \"Two-entry loop predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark spends most of its control-flow budget in a small number of\n+  hot backedges. The earlier one-entry loop predictor was too narrow,\n+  while broader BTB/BHT and call/return schemes paid too much in fetch-\n+  side bookkeeping and Fmax loss.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a tiny two-entry tagged loop predictor in `rtl/if_stage.sv` that\n+  learns trip counts for backward conditional branches and predicts the\n+  loop backedge until the learned exit. Keep JAL/JALR fully reactive in\n+  EX and do not add a general BTB, BHT, or return stack. Two entries\n+  should cover the hottest CoreMark loops without the aliasing and\n+  timing overhead that hurt the broader predictor attempts.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a small tagged two-entry loop predictor for backward conditional branches and use it to steer fetch on the hottest loop backedges.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-003-r10s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-003-r10s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-003-r10s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/benc