{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778996221074,"sessionID":"ses_1cb90524fffe4X5tPgV31C1yHp","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_Zy7tyor8KpG50UGZQzlGGcws","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-003-r12s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260517-003-r12s2\n+title: \"Two-entry backward predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark still spends most of its control-flow budget on a small set of\n+  repeated loop backedges. The last successful hypothesis showed that a very\n+  narrow backward-branch predictor can improve fitness without dragging in the\n+  BTB, RAS, or JAL/JALR bookkeeping that hurt broader predictor attempts.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a tiny two-entry tagged predictor in `rtl/if_stage.sv` that only learns\n+  backward conditional branches. Each entry should remember the branch PC,\n+  target, and a small taken-confidence counter so fetch can redirect early on\n+  the hottest loop backedges while cold branches still fall back to the current\n+  EX-stage recovery path. Keep JAL, JALR, and forward branches reactive so the\n+  predictor stays narrow and cheap to time.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a tiny two-entry tagged predictor for backward conditional branches, with learned target/confidence state and EX redirect recovery for misses.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-003-r12s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-003-r12s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-003-r12s2.yaml\