{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1779001705266,"sessionID":"ses_1cb3e1b4affe8q0PeAfrfawTEf","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_iBb3ZtnnAiySC6Uyqv1Y3409","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-003-r15s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260517-003-r15s2\n+title: \"Hot-loop shadow latch\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark already proved that this core benefits from predicting loop\n+  backedges, but the broader predictor experiments lost ground to aliasing,\n+  bookkeeping, and fetch-side fanout. A narrower predictor that keeps only\n+  the hottest backedge resident should preserve the useful loop win while\n+  cutting the logic that hurt the larger table-based attempts.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Keep the current backward-branch prediction policy, but add a single-entry\n+  shadow latch for the hottest taken backedge so fetch can reuse a remembered\n+  PC/target pair on the next visit instead of rebuilding it every time. Do not\n+  add a general BTB, confidence table, or JAL/JALR speculation; the goal is\n+  to preserve the proven loop-backedge win while trimming the common-path\n+  fetch cone and avoiding the aliasing and bookkeeping that hurt broader\n+  predictor variants.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a single-entry hot-backedge shadow latch and use it to bypass repeated work for the hottest loop while leaving the existing EX recovery path intact.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-003-r15s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-003-r15s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tourname